    "result_text": "Done. The build passes with all tests green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec: *a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.*\n\n`membershipLevel` is a derived value (`MembershipLevel.of(owner)`, computed from points). To make the cap stick and be returned on `GET`, I persist a ceiling at creation time:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added a `membershipLevelCap` (Integer) field with getter/setter and `membership_level_cap` column, mirroring the other creation-time derived fields.\n2. **Schema** \u2014 added `membership_level_cap INTEGER` to all four dialects (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2/mysql/postgres). The test profile is `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the SQL schema change is what actually applies.\n3. **`MembershipLevel.of(owner)`** \u2014 now bounds the points-derived level: `cap == null ? level : min(level, cap)`.\n4. **New step `AssignMembershipLevelCap`** \u2014 runs after `AssignHouseholdSize` and before save; scans existing (non-deleted) owners sharing the `householdId`, and sets the cap to `maxMemberLevel + 1`. With no existing member, the cap stays `null` (uncapped). Wired into `owners.POST.yml`.\n\n## One necessary behavioral change\n\nThe new test creates two owners in the same household (same lastName + postcode) and requires **both** to succeed, then checks the second's level \u2264 first + 1. The existing `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` step rejected the second household member with **409**, so the test could never reach the level assertion. This checkpoint's rule makes household membership first-class (households *have members* with capped levels), superseding the hard duplicate rejection \u2014 so I removed the `checkIdentity` step from the POST pipeline and rewired `assignHousehold \u2192 assignHouseholdSize`. The exception/handler/class remain in the tree (unused, harmless); no other code or test referenced the 409 behavior.\n\nVerified in the run: owner A \u2192 `membershipLevel=1` (uncapped, first in household), owner B \u2192 raw level 2 capped to `membershipLevel=2` (\u2264 1+1).",
